# Project State & Continuity Artifacts

Five files at the repo root track project state. Know which is durable spec-adjacent
record versus local working memory, and respect each one's write cadence.

**All five are tracked in git.** They are committed (not gitignored) so that cloud agents —
which only ever see the repo through their own branch and cannot read a local machine's
untracked files — can read and update the shared project state and participate on equal
footing. The "durable record vs local working memory" distinction below is about audience
and write cadence, not git-tracking: `SCRATCHPAD*` are still ephemeral working memory that
gets overwritten/compacted, they just travel with the branch.

| File | Role | Tracked in git? | Write pattern |
|---|---|---|---|
| `OPEN_ISSUES.md` | Current, active issues ONLY — zero history | yes | edit in place |
| `PLANNING.md` | Milestone plan with status board and exit criteria | yes | edit in place |
| `SCRATCHPAD.json` | Compact live snapshot of current focus | yes | overwrite in place |
| `OPEN_ISSUES_LOG.jsonl` | Append-only history of all issue changes | yes | append, ≤1/turn |
| `SCRATCHPAD_LOG.jsonl` | Append-only changelog of work/state per turn | yes | append, ≤1/turn |

## OPEN_ISSUES.md — current and active only, never history

The file contains **only issues that are open right now**. No resolved lists, no
"recently fixed" notes, no progress narratives, no status updates inside entries. All
history — openings, edits, resolutions with a one-line note — lives exclusively in
`OPEN_ISSUES_LOG.jsonl`.

- **Resolving:** delete the entire entry and record the resolution in the log the same
  turn. Do not leave tombstones or header mentions.
- **Numbering:** stable and historical — numbers are never reused or renumbered. The
  header carries a single `Next issue number: N` counter; when adding an issue, take `N`
  and increment the counter. Gaps in the sequence are expected and carry no meaning.
- **Entry content:** only the remaining work, its motivation, and pointers (SI sections,
  file paths). If an entry starts accumulating "done so far" prose, move that to the log
  and trim the entry back to what remains.

## PLANNING.md

The milestone sequence, exit criteria, and a **status board** near the top (one line per
milestone: `not started` / `in progress` / `blocked` / `done`). Update the board when a
milestone starts, blocks, or lands — statuses live only on the board, not scattered in
milestone bodies. Reference issue numbers; never restate issue text. When exit criteria
are met, mark `done` and resolve the corresponding issues (via the OPEN_ISSUES protocol
above) in the same turn.

## SCRATCHPAD.json

Minimal, compact, agent-facing continuity memory — NOT a document for humans. Its job is
to let the orchestrator (and any subagent handed it) recover context after a compaction,
distraction, network failure, or fresh resume. The orchestrator owns and maintains it;
subagents primarily read it (pass the relevant slice into subagent prompts). It is a
snapshot, not a log: overwrite in place, and keep it valid JSON at all times.

**Hard limits (enforced at every write; numeric values are agent-adjustable within the
ranges in the rule-governance rule — the *existence* of the limits is not):**

- Total file size ≤ **4,000 characters** (~1k tokens — cheap to inject into any prompt).
- Each array (`recent`, `discoveries`, `open_threads`, `blockers`) ≤ **5 items**,
  newest last; adding a sixth evicts the oldest.
- Each string item ≤ **200 characters**. Summarize; don't paste.
- Increment `turn` on every write. Run a **compaction pass every 10 turns** (i.e. when
  `turn % 10 == 0`) *and* immediately whenever the size cap is breached: re-justify every
  item, drop anything stale, merge duplicates, and **promote durable content out** —
  discoveries to `OPEN_ISSUES.md` or the SI, finished work to `SCRATCHPAD_LOG.jsonl`.
  Nothing should survive two consecutive compactions unchanged; promote it or delete it.

### Template

```json
{
  "updated_at": "2026-07-23T21:04-05:00",
  "turn": 17,
  "focus": { "milestone": "M1", "issue": 27, "goal": "canonicalize Q-score in SI S2.6.1" },
  "recent": ["promoted W_v formulas from reference note", "circle regression still green"],
  "discoveries": ["Lindeberg response is self-normalizing at equilibrium (see #28)"],
  "open_threads": ["null-hypothesis test not yet wired into clustering.py"],
  "next_step": "add single-cluster null as first-class candidate",
  "blockers": []
}
```

### Keys

| Key | Type | Purpose / justification |
|---|---|---|
| `updated_at` | string (ISO 8601) | Staleness check — a reader can tell if the snapshot predates recent work. |
| `turn` | integer | Monotone write counter; drives the every-10-turns compaction cadence and shows snapshot age. |
| `focus` | object | The one thing in progress; the anchor after a disruption. |
| `focus.milestone` | string | `PLANNING.md` milestone id (e.g. `"M1"`); ties work to the plan. |
| `focus.issue` | integer \| null | Active `OPEN_ISSUES.md` number, or `null` if none. |
| `focus.goal` | string | The immediate objective in one phrase. |
| `recent` | string[] | Last few meaningful actions/decisions, newest last; rebuilds momentum. |
| `discoveries` | string[] | Non-obvious findings expensive to rediscover; cite issue/SI refs. |
| `open_threads` | string[] | Started-but-unfinished work and unresolved questions. |
| `next_step` | string | The single next action — what to do first on resume. |
| `blockers` | string[] | Pending user choices or external blocks; empty `[]` when clear. |

## The append-only logs

Both `.jsonl` files are append-only and written **at most once per turn**. Append a single
consolidated entry near the end of a turn only if something changed; if nothing changed,
write nothing. Never rewrite or delete prior lines. Keep entries compact (one line each).

- `OPEN_ISSUES_LOG.jsonl` — the **sole** history of `OPEN_ISSUES.md`: issues added,
  edited, and resolved (with a one-line resolution note). If you need to know what was
  fixed and when, look here — never in `OPEN_ISSUES.md` itself.
- `SCRATCHPAD_LOG.jsonl` — summarizes the turn's work / notable state changes (the running
  trail behind the ephemeral `SCRATCHPAD.json`; also the destination for finished-work
  items evicted during scratchpad compaction).

Suggested compact schema (keep to one line):

```json
{"ts":"2026-07-23T21:04-05:00","summary":"Q-score canonicalized in SI S2.6.1","added":[],"edited":[27],"resolved":[{"n":30,"note":"dip test replaced by Q-gated recursion"}]}
```

```json
{"ts":"2026-07-23T21:04-05:00","focus":"M1 clustering","summary":"promoted W_v formulas from reference note; circle regression still green","next":"wire null-hypothesis test"}
```
